  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...    \nBy late Saturday evening, conditions begin to align for a longer \nduration and more widespread rainfall event through Sunday. The \naforementioned subtropical jet streak is shown by global guidance \ncontracting and cyclonically curving around the base of the broader \ntroughing off the West Coast, as it begins to progress eastward. \nStrong dynamical ascent mechanisms and a prolonged period of \nsouthwesterly upslope flow over the Central AZ high terrain will \nalign with excellent, deep moisture through the atmospheric column. \nImpressively high PWATs are still forecast, in excess of 1.00-1.25\" \nand near the maximum of climatology for early November. NAEFS/ENS \nmean IVT values are consistent with an inland penetrating \natmospheric river and are also, unsurprisingly, near climatological \nmaximum values for the time of year (peaking around 300-600 \nkg/(ms)). However, midlevel lapse rates will be poor and thus \ninstability will be very limited, with MUCAPEs likely never \nexceeding 200-300 J/kg. We expect showers to blossom first over the \nhigh terrain northeast of Phoenix sometime in the evening Saturday \nbefore filling in across most of south-central Arizona around or \njust after midnight. Showers will stay somewhat scattered through \nthe overnight hours before becoming widespread over much of Arizona \nby mid morning Sunday. As the main shortwave begins to track through \nsouthern California later Sunday morning, a fairly solid shield of \nlight to moderate rain should move through southern California and \nthen through Arizona Sunday afternoon. Upslope showers should \npersist over portions of south-central Arizona into the high terrain \ninto Sunday evening, but the activity should be gradually winding \ndown.\n\nExpected rainfall rates on Sunday are not all that high, but rain \ntotals will add up over time as this event will last longer than our \ntypical weather events. Based on the record moisture levels, strong \nforcing, but little instability rainfall rates will generally be in \nthe 0.1-0.2\"/hr range in the Phoenix area to as high as 0.2-0.3\"/hr \nin the high terrain north and east of the Phoenix area. Fairly \nsteady light to moderate rainfall is expected from just before \nsunrise through the afternoon in the aforementioned areas with \nrainfall totals averaging 1-1.5\" in Phoenix to 2-3\" over the higher \nterrain. Believe it our not, the NBM is also suggesting the \npossibility (25%) of rainfall totals over 2\" in the Phoenix to as \nmuch as 3-4\" in the high terrain. Much lower rainfall totals of 0.25-\n0.5\" are expected over the lower deserts of southwest Arizona and \nsoutheast California due to some rain-shadowing effects and the lack \nof upslope forcing.\n\nEven if the average forecast rainfall totals come to fruition on \nSunday, the bulk of the rain will runoff as it will be falling on \nalready saturated soils. In turn, another Flood Watch has been \nissued for a large area of south-central AZ, and the eastern AZ high \nterrain starting overnight Saturday night and lasting into early \nMonday morning. During this time we anticipate the potential for \nmany of the washes filling or overflowing, and flooding in some of \nthe larger creeks and rivers as all of the expected rain works \nthrough the water basins.\n\nDry air will begin to filter into the region from the northwest \nlater on Sunday, overspreading the bulk of the area by Monday \nmorning. We may have some lingering showers across the eastern 1/3rd \nof Arizona Sunday night into Monday morning, but additional rainfall \namounts should be fairly light. The rest of Monday will bring \ndecreasing clouds as the drier air works into the region while \nanother follow-on shortwave trough moves southeastward into northern \nNew Mexico. Temperatures for both Sunday and Monday will be fairly \ncool with highs as low as the lower 70s for the Phoenix area to \nmaybe the mid to upper 70s across the western lower deserts. Upper \nlevel ridging is then forecast to mostly take over across the Desert \nSouthwest going into the middle part of next week, but temperatures \nwill be fairly slow to rise with readings staying below normal.",
